Since Java 9, interfaces can have private methods. These methods are mainly used as helper methods for other methods inside the interface.

You cannot call them directly from outside the interface or from an implementing class.

Example :
    interface Vehicle {

        default void start() {
            checkEngine();
            System.out.println("Vehicle started");
        }

        default void stop() {
            checkEngine();
            System.out.println("Vehicle stopped");
        }

        private void checkEngine() {
            System.out.println("Checking engine...");
        }
    }

Here, both start() and stop() use the same private helper method. Calling it

    class Car implements Vehicle {

    }

    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Car c = new Car();

            c.start();
            c.stop();
        }
    }

Output:
    Checking engine...
    Vehicle started
    Checking engine...
    Vehicle stopped
    Can we do this?
    c.checkEngine(); // ERROR


Because checkEngine() is private to the interface.

Why are private methods useful?

Suppose multiple default methods need the same logic:

interface Payment {

    default void pay() {
        validate();
        System.out.println("Payment successful");
    }

    default void refund() {
        validate();
        System.out.println("Refund successful");
    }

    private void validate() {
        System.out.println("Validating payment...");
    }
}

Instead of duplicating validation code in every default method, we keep it in one private helper.

Important point

Private interface methods:

Introduced in Java 9
Must have a body
Cannot be accessed by implementing classes
Cannot be overridden
Can be called only from methods inside the same interface

Think of them as internal helper methods used by default or static methods of the interface.